  "body": "feat(multiaxis): stated rotary axis from the machine line, with a disagreed-face refusal\n\n## What changed\n\nIssue #2772, parts 1 and 2 of the plan in\n`docs/decisions/2772-rotary-4axis-refusal.md`. The rotary kinds\n(Rotary Contour, Rotary Parallel, Rotary Offset) read their rotation\naxis from a STATEMENT first; today's free fit is the fallback, and\nnothing stated keeps every behaviour bit for bit.\n\n1. The stated-axis resolver, `camcraft_app::rotary_stated` over\n   `camcraft_motion::multiaxis::axis_stated`:\n   - the op's own `stated_axis` direction first, else the mounted\n     machine's stated rotary lines (a stated `axis` vector, else the\n     RS-274 letter's line - the index-swing clause's own\n     `rotary_line` reading, so one law reads both doors);\n   - each line anchored through the setup's work offset origin (the\n     indexed post's setup law: the WCS sits on the rotary\n     centreline), canonicalized the fit's own way (signed, pinned to\n     the `o . u = 0` foot), so a part truly on the line keeps the\n     fitted path move for move;\n   - JUDGED, never trusted: the fit's own concurrency share,\n     coarseness skip and bearing law about the stated line\n     (`axis_stated::judged_any`). A disagreement refuses by name:\n     the part's own fitted axis and the two acts that fix it when\n     the model is a revolve placed off the line; the freeform's\n     4-axis wording (with the pinned \"share no rotation axis\"\n     words) when no axis fits at all. Never a silent fall back to a\n     free fit when a line was stated. A stated line that leans or is\n     no direction refuses by name, as the swing clause does.\n2. The caller switch, stated first / fit fallback:\n   - `fixture::rotary_axis_of` - feeds the sim's chord pass\n     (`stock_sim_rotary::rotary_plan`), the first traverse and the\n     post gate's struck-steel law (`fixture_struck`): all three\n     follow with no change of their own;\n   - `first_traverse::turned_axis` - already prefers the job axis,\n     which is now the stated-or-fitted line;\n   - `rotary_law::rotary_into` - the radial field verdict takes the\n     job's resolved axis (`rotary_axis`), keeping its fail-closed\n     Gouge for the nothing-known case;\n   - `rotary_picks::usable` and the centre-cutting audit's\n     `revolved_bar_stated` - judge and read the resolved axis;\n   - the letter door `rotary_gate::letter_gate` (moved from\n     generate.rs) - a stated line answers the letter without a fit,\n     held to the machine inventory;\n   - the three motion kernels grow `*_stated` entries\n     (`rotary_contour_stated`, `rotary_parallel_over_stated`,\n     `rotary_offset_stated`) that take the judged axis; the plain\n     entries keep the fit inside, so turning, drilling and every\n     existing golden path are untouched.\n3. The op param: `stated_axis` on the three arms - a direction\n   ratio, never unit-scaled (the machine file's own law for its\n   `axis` vector), through the WCS origin, overriding the machine\n   line. UI edit (the rotary page's Passes tab, an armed group of\n   three direction numbers riding the existing EditOperationParams\n   commit), MCP patch (`edit_operation`'s merge patch over the DTO,\n   automatic), project round-trip (`stated_axis`, PROJECT_VERSION\n   steps v77 -> v78: an older reader must refuse rather than drop\n   the statement and turn the part about an axis the user never\n   named).\n4. The cache stamp: the op's own direction, the machine's stated\n   lines and the WCS anchor enter the digest ONLY when a line is\n   stated - no cached path of a document without one goes stale.\n\n## The readings\n\n- The WCS origin is the stated line's anchor: the post subtracts the\n  work offset from world coordinates, so in program coordinates the\n  rotary centreline passes through the WCS origin - the same law the\n  index-swing clause and the lathe spindle law read.\n- The judge is the fit's own (`axis.rs`'s `data_of` + `judge`): the\n  tolerance, the 90% concurrency share, the coarseness skip and the\n  bearing law, evaluated about the stated line instead of a candidate\n  from the eigen search. On the reporter's helical rotor the judge\n  fails and the radial raster stays LOCKED (the decision doc's law:\n  its radial pose can gouge on helical lobes) - the disagreement\n  refusal says so and names the 4-axis case.\n- A machine whose work-turning rotary states no `axis` vector keeps\n  today's fit (absent is not stated, never zero). A trunnion's\n  work-turning pair is read in chain order and the first line the\n  model holds wins, so a standing revolve takes the C and a lying\n  one takes the A.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\n- motion `axis_stated_tests`: a revolve holds its stated line and\n  reads the fitted axis bit for bit; the canonical line is signed\n  and pinned; a stated line away from the part names the part's own\n  axis; the helical screw refuses with the 4-axis wording; an empty\n  mesh refuses like the fit.\n- app `rotary_stated_axis`:\n  `a_stated_machine_line_generates_the_fitted_path` (microARC 4 +\n  laid revolve: move-for-move the fitted program),\n  `the_millturns_stated_c_carries_the_standing_bar` (C answered\n  without a fit, the stamp runs),\n  `the_screw_rotor_on_a_stated_A_is_refused_by_name` (the radial\n  kinds stay locked on the screw),\n  `the_screw_rotor_without_a_stated_line_keeps_the_old_refusal`\n  (the pinned \"share no rotation axis\" words untouched),\n  `a_stated_line_away_from_the_part_names_the_parts_own_axis` (both\n  the disagreement and the letter-door wordings),\n  `a_stated_line_the_part_holds_on_a_machine_that_has_it_generates`\n  (the op's own statement, either sign),\n  `a_work_offset_move_moves_the_stated_line_and_refuses` (the\n  anchor is the WCS's own position),\n  `the_stated_axis_round_trips_the_project_file` (v78 round-trip,\n  v77 steps up unstated),\n  `a_v77_file_opens_and_a_newer_file_is_refused` (the version pin).\n- app `rotary_gate`: `a_stated_line_answers_the_letter_without_a_fit`\n  (the screw's fit refuses, the stated line still answers).\n- app `rotary_field_parity`:\n  `the_stated_axis_parallel_reads_the_radial_law` - the heavy-lane\n  parity of the stated-A program (the field eye vs the radial law,\n  `#[ignore]`d like its three siblings; the quick suite pins the\n  path equality, this lane proves the stated axis flows the whole\n  sim pipeline at the verdict class).\n- The continuous 4-axis example regenerated at v78 (its op follows\n  the microARC's stated line - the same path the fit reads).\n\n## What was NOT exercised\n\n- The heavy parity lane runs at a cut, not here (the quick gate\n  pins the path equality instead).\n- No UI pixel snapshot is touched (release-lane only).\n\n## Not included (the issue stays open)\n\n- The RADIAL DROP FINISH KERNEL and the radial-step roughing\n  (decision part 3/4): the live claim on\n  `mkinney:issue/2772-radial-drop` holds that work. This PR's\n  disagreement refusal keeps the radial kinds locked on helical\n  parts until that kernel can consume the stated axis safely.\n- No 360-degree radial drop finish for Rotary Parallel, no\n  Doosan/Fanuc posting of one - same slice.\n\nChangelog: Rotary Contour, Rotary Parallel and Rotary Offset read a\nstated rotation axis first (the operation's own direction, else the\nmachine's stated rotary line through the work offset origin), judged\nagainst the model's faces - a part on the stated line cuts the fitted\npath unchanged, a part whose faces disagree refuses by name (the\n4-axis case named), and nothing stated keeps the fitted axis (v78\nproject files).\n\nfork synced at v0.25.0\n\nRefs #2772\n",
